<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-antiderivatives">
    <title>Antiderivatives</title>

    <section xml:id="sec-antiderivatives">
        <title>Antiderivatives</title>

        <paragraphs xml:id="par-antiderivative-introduction">
            <title>Motivation</title>
            <p> In differential calculus (Math 7A), we learn how to find the <em>rate of change</em>
                (derivative) of a function. Integral calculus (Math 7B) asks the reverse question:
                if we know a rate of change, can we recover the <em>original quantity</em>? </p>

            <p>
                Examples from the natural sciences include:
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>growth rate <m>\to</m> population size,</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>reaction rate <m>\to</m> amount of product formed,</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>velocity <m>\to</m> position.</p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </paragraphs>

        <paragraphs xml:id="par-derivative-review">
            <title>A brief review of basic derivatives</title>

            <p>
                Antidifferentiation reverses differentiation. The derivative rules
                most useful in this section are summarized below.
            </p>

            <table xml:id="tab-basic-derivatives">
                <title>Basic derivatives</title>
                <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor">
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>
                            <m>F(x)</m>
                        </cell>
                        <cell>
                            <m>F'(x)</m>
                        </cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>
                            <m>x^n</m>
                        </cell>
                        <cell>
                            <m>nx^{n-1}</m>
                        </cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>
                            <m>e^x</m>
                        </cell>
                        <cell>
                            <m>e^x</m>
                        </cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>
                            <m>\ln\lvert x\rvert</m>
                        </cell>
                        <cell>
                            <m>1/x</m>
                        </cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>
                            <m>\sin x</m>
                        </cell>
                        <cell>
                            <m>\cos x</m>
                        </cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>
                            <m>\cos x</m>
                        </cell>
                        <cell>
                            <m>-\sin x</m>
                        </cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <p>
                We will also use the constant-multiple rule and the chain rule with
                a linear inside function. For example,
            </p>

            <me>
                \frac{d}{dx}\sin(2x)=2\cos(2x).
            </me>
        </paragraphs>

        <activity xml:id="act-poll-antiderivatives">
            <title>Poll: Reversing a derivative</title>

            <statement>
                <p> Which of the following functions have derivative <m>6x^2</m>? Select all that
                    apply. </p>

                <ol marker="A">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>2x^3</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>6x^3</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>2x^3+5</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>3x^2</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p> Both <m>2x^3</m> and <m>2x^3+5</m> have derivative <m>6x^2</m>. More generally,
                    every function of the form <m>2x^3+C</m>, where <m>C</m> is any constant, has
                    derivative <m>6x^2</m>. </p>
            </solution>
        </activity>

        <definition xml:id="def-antiderivative">
            <title>Antiderivative</title>
            <statement>
                <p> A function <m>F(x)</m> is an antiderivative of <m>f(x)</m> if </p>
                <me>
                    F'(x)=f(x).
                </me>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <example xml:id="ex-antiderivative-x2">
            <title>An antiderivative of <m>x^2</m></title>

            <statement>
                <p> Find an antiderivative of <m>f(x)=x^2</m>. </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p> A first guess might be <m>F(x)=x^3</m>, but </p>

                <me>
                    F'(x)=3x^2,
                </me>

                <p> so this is not an antiderivative of <m>x^2</m>. Instead, consider </p>

                <me>
                    F(x)=\frac{1}{3}x^3.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Differentiating gives
                </p>

                <me>
                    F'(x)=\frac{1}{3}(3x^2)=x^2.
                </me>

                <p> Therefore <m>\frac{1}{3}x^3</m> is an antiderivative of <m>x^2</m>. </p>

                <p>
                    It is not the only one. Adding any constant does not change
                    the derivative, so the general antiderivative is
                </p>

                <me>
                    F(x)=\frac{1}{3}x^3+C,
                </me>

                <p> where <m>C</m> is an arbitrary constant. </p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <remark xml:id="rem-constant-of-integration">
            <title>The constant of integration</title>
            <p> If <m>F</m> is an antiderivative of <m>f</m>, then <m>F(x)+C</m> is also an
                antiderivative for every constant <m>C</m>. We therefore include a constant of
                integration when writing a general antiderivative. </p>
        </remark>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-basic-antiderivative-rules">
        <title>Basic Antiderivative Rules</title>

        <p>
            The basic derivative rules can be read in reverse to obtain
            antiderivative rules.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="tab-basic-antiderivatives">
            <title>Basic antiderivative rules</title>
            <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor">
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Function</cell>
                    <cell>General antiderivative</cell>
                    <cell>Condition</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>
                        <m>x^n</m>
                    </cell>
                    <cell>
                        <m>\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}+C</m>
                    </cell>
                    <cell>
                        <m>n\ne -1</m>
                    </cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>
                        <m>\dfrac{1}{x}</m>
                    </cell>
                    <cell>
                        <m>\ln\lvert x\rvert+C</m>
                    </cell>
                    <cell>
                        <m>x\ne 0</m>
                    </cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>
                        <m>e^x</m>
                    </cell>
                    <cell>
                        <m>e^x+C</m>
                    </cell>
                    <cell />
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>
                        <m>\cos x</m>
                    </cell>
                    <cell>
                        <m>\sin x+C</m>
                    </cell>
                    <cell />
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>
                        <m>\sin x</m>
                    </cell>
                    <cell>
                        <m>-\cos x+C</m>
                    </cell>
                    <cell />
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
        <p> Antiderivatives may be found term by term, and constant multiples may be factored out.
            In particular, if <m>F'(x)=f(x)</m> and <m>G'(x)=g(x)</m>, then </p>

        <md>
            <mrow>
                cf(x) &amp;\longleftrightarrow cF(x),
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
                f(x)+g(x) &amp;\longleftrightarrow F(x)+G(x).
            </mrow>
        </md>

        <p>
            Here, the expression on the right is an antiderivative of the
            expression on the left.
</p>

        <warning xml:id="warn-power-rule-minus-one">
            <title>The case <m>n=-1</m></title>

            <p> The power rule for antiderivatives does not apply when <m>n=-1</m>, because it would
                require division by zero. Instead, </p>

            <me>
                \frac{1}{x}=x^{-1}
                \qquad\text{has general antiderivative}\qquad
                \ln\lvert x\rvert+C.
            </me>
        </warning>

        <example xml:id="ex-linear-inside-antiderivatives">
            <title>Accounting for a constant factor</title>

            <statement>
                <p>Find the general antiderivative of <m>f(x)=\cos(2x)</m>.</p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p> We know that the derivative of <m>\sin(2x)</m> is <m>2\cos(2x)</m>. This is
                    twice the function we want. Multiplying by <m>1/2</m> compensates for this extra
                    factor: </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{d}{dx}\left(\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)\right)
                    =\frac{1}{2}\big(2\cos(2x)\big)
                    =\cos(2x).
                </me>

                <p>Therefore, the general antiderivative is</p>

                <me>
                    F(x)=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)+C.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-general-antiderivative">
            <title>Finding a general antiderivative</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the general antiderivative of
                </p>

                <me>
                    f(x)=6x^2+\cos(2x)-\frac{1}{5x}.
                </me>
                <p>
                    Try to find the antiderivative before reading the solution.
                    Remember to check your answer by differentiating.
                </p>

            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    We antidifferentiate term by term.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Since
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{d}{dx}(2x^3)=6x^2,
                </me>

                <me>
                    \frac{d}{dx}\left(\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)\right)=\cos(2x),
                </me>

                <p>
                    and
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{d}{dx}\ln\lvert x\rvert=\frac{1}{x},
                </me>

                <p>
                    the general antiderivative is
                </p>

                <me>
                    F(x)=2x^3+\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)
                    -\frac{1}{5}\ln\lvert x\rvert+C.
                </me>

                <p>
                    We check by differentiating:
</p>

                <me>
                    F'(x)
                    =6x^2+\cos(2x)-\frac{1}{5x}
                    =f(x).
</me>

            </solution>
        </example>


        <remark xml:id="rem-check-by-differentiating">
            <title>Checking your answer</title>
            <p>
                A useful way to check an antiderivative is to differentiate
                your answer. You should recover the original function.
            </p>
        </remark>
    </section>


    <section xml:id="sec-initial-value-problems">
        <title>Differential Equations and Initial Value Problems</title>

        <p>
            Antidifferentiation can be used to solve simple differential
            equations.
        </p>

        <example xml:id="ex-simple-de">
            <title>Solving a differential equation by antidifferentiation</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the general solution of
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{3}{x^2},
                    \qquad x\ne 0.
                </me>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p> Rewrite the right-hand side as <m>3x^{-2}</m>. Antidifferentiating gives </p>
                <me>
                    y=\frac{3x^{-1}}{-1}+C
                    =-3x^{-1}+C
                    =-\frac{3}{x}+C.
</me>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <definition xml:id="def-initial-value-problem">
            <title>Initial value problem</title>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    An initial value problem consists of a differential equation
                    together with a specified value of the solution at a particular
                    point. The additional condition can be used to determine the
                    constant of integration.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <example xml:id="ex-ivp-population">
            <title>An initial value problem</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{dN}{dt}=t,
                    \qquad t\ge 0,
                </me>

                <p>
                    subject to the initial condition
                </p>

                <me>
                    N(0)=20.
                </me>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    First find the general solution:
                </p>

                <me>
                    N(t)=\frac{1}{2}t^2+C.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Now use the initial condition:
                </p>

                <me>
                    20=N(0)=\frac{1}{2}(0)^2+C.
                </me>

                <p> Therefore <m>C=20</m>, and the particular solution is </p>

                <me>
                    N(t)=\frac{1}{2}t^2+20.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ivp-family">
            <caption>A family of solutions and the solution selected by the initial condition.</caption>

            <image source="images/antiderivatives-ivp-soln.jpeg" width="60%"
                alt="Hand-drawn sketch illustrating an antiderivative." />
        </figure>

        <p> The initial condition selects one member of the family of general solutions, as shown in <xref
                ref="fig-ivp-family" />. </p>

    </section>

</chapter>